    <h2 class="sec-title">1. Problem Statement</h2>
    <h3 class="sub-title">1.1 Context & Background</h3>
    <p>Varendra University (VU) has shifted its primary academic activities to its permanent campus located at <strong>Kharkhari, Rajshahi</strong>, situated along the northern bypass corridor. Consequently, students, professors, and administrative staff commute daily from various residential, commercial, and transit zones across Rajshahi City—such as <strong>Zero Point, Laxmipur, Railgate, Bhadra, Talaimari, Binodpur, and Naodapara</strong>.</p>
    <p>In Rajshahi, commuters rely predominantly on battery-run auto-rickshaws (Easybikes) and local tempos. Commuting presents daily challenges because multiple paths exist to reach the university, with major trade-offs between monetary fare (in BDT) and travel time (in minutes).</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">1.2 The Problem to Solve</h3>
    <p>A student's priority varies depending on the situation:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Fastest Route ("Taratari jawa jabe"):</strong> During morning exams or tight class schedules, students prioritize minimizing travel time, even if it costs slightly more via express bypass routes.</li>
        <li><strong>Cheapest Route ("Vara kom lagbe"):</strong> On normal days, students on a limited monthly budget prefer lower fares via shared local tempos, even if it requires extra travel time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="sub-title">1.3 Project Objective</h3>
    <p>To design and implement an efficient, automated decision-support system in C++ using <strong>Dijkstra's Shortest Path Algorithm</strong>. Given any starting location in Rajshahi and the chosen priority (Cheapest vs. Fastest), the system calculates the mathematically optimal step-by-step route to Varendra University.</p>

    <h2 class="sec-title">2. Algorithm of the Solution</h2>
    <h3 class="sub-title">2.1 Step-by-Step Algorithm (Dijkstra with Priority Selector)</h3>
    <div class="code-box">
1. Model the Rajshahi road network as an undirected graph G = (V, E).<br>
   - Vertices (V): Transit hubs (Zero Point, Railgate, Talaimari, VU, etc.)<br>
   - Edges (E): Direct road segments with dual weights: {fare, time}.<br>
2. Input starting location and priority (1 = Cheapest, 2 = Fastest).<br>
   Destination is set to "Varendra University".<br>
3. Initialize minCost array with INFINITY, parent array with -1.<br>
   Set minCost[startNode] = 0 and push (0, startNode) to Min-Priority Queue (PQ).<br>
4. While PQ is not empty:<br>
   a. Extract node 'u' with minimum cost.<br>
   b. If currentCost > minCost[u], continue.<br>
   c. If u == endNode, break.<br>
   d. For each neighbor edge (u -> v):<br>
        weight = (priority == 1) ? edge.fare : edge.time<br>
        If minCost[u] + weight < minCost[v]:<br>
            minCost[v] = minCost[u] + weight<br>
            parent[v] = u<br>
            Push (minCost[v], v) to PQ.<br>
5. Backtrack from endNode using parent[] array to reconstruct the optimal route.<br>
6. Traverse the path to sum individual fares and transit times; display the breakdown.
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub-title">2.2 Algorithm Flowchart</h3>
    <p><strong>[User Input: Start & Priority] &rarr; [Initialize Dijkstra: minCost=INF, PQ] &rarr; [Extract Min Node u] &rarr; [Select Weight: Fare/Time] &rarr; [Relax Neighbor Edges] &rarr; [Backtrack via parent[]] &rarr; [Display Optimal Route & Stats]</strong></p>

