    <h2 class="sec-title">5. Discussion</h2>
    <h3 class="sub-title">5.1 Time and Space Complexity</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Time Complexity: O((V + E) log V)</strong> &mdash; Min-heap priority queue extracts minimum cost vertices and relaxes edges in logarithmic time. With |V|=10 and |E|=16, the code executes in less than 1 millisecond.</li>
        <li><strong>Space Complexity: O(V + E)</strong> &mdash; Graph is stored in an adjacency list with linear space overhead for tracking arrays (minCost, parent).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="sub-title">5.2 Technical Challenges</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Dual-Criteria Weight Management:</strong> Dijkstra natively operates on a single metric. Combining fare and time into a single sum causes unit conflicts (Taka vs. Minutes). We resolved this by dynamically choosing the edge weight based on user priority while independently summing the secondary metric along the reconstructed path.</li>
        <li><strong>Path Backtracking:</strong> Reconstructing the full sequence of intermediate transit stops was achieved using a predecessor <code>parent[]</code> array.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="sub-title">5.3 Project Limitations</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Static weights represent average transit times and do not capture sudden peak-hour traffic jams.</li>
        <li>Assumes public easybike boarding occurs at designated crossings.</li>
    </ul>

